\f2\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs26 Alexander Shakti was not an unintelligent man, some said quite the opposite was true, although these people were few and far between. His capacity to engage in abstract thought was minimal. He was socially retarded, romantically inept and had no ability or, perhaps, desire to embrace any meaningful discourse. A conversation with Alex, as he liked to be called, was an awkward blend of pregnant pauses and obscure humour, usually ending after a few excruciating minutes. Invariably there was some ruse employed by the victim in a desperate attempt at extricating themselves. \'93Is that the time?\'94 or \'93I\'92ve got a meeting\'94 or, simply, \'93Please don\'92t have children.\'94 Alex had heard them all before, but oddly enough, he did not realise that he was a social pariah and had no idea he was a target of derision and ridicule. Such was his ego and unwavering confidence in his abilities, he held the strong belief that he was cool and well liked.\
Born into a comfortable level 5 home he became a disappointment and later, a total embarrassment, to his hardworking professional parents. Although his mother was born level 2, she had owned three houses and two apartments by the time she was 20 years old. By age twenty-two, she was running her own Accommodation Directorate and the fact that she managed to land a level 5 husband, whilst extremely rare, was no accident. With pure force of will she took on larger and by reputation, more ferocious competitors and crushed them beneath her stylish heels. When her business was publicly listed, there were protestations from the Stock Exchange directly to the Ministry of Moral Compliance. Although there was no legal impediment, it seemed that a Level 2 CEO could not list a company on the Stock Exchange, they simply would not have it. Before any public trading on her stock commenced, however, she was made an offer she could not refuse, the sale of the company at an inflated price and allocation with an upwardly mobile level 4. She knew that with her talent she and her new husband would be at level 5 in double quick time and their children would want for nothing, breaking into the senior executive ranks \'96 level 7 and above.\
	\
The home they created for Alex was the picture perfect environment for the budding young corporate raider. Every activity engaged in; every toy played with; every vid-stick watched and playgroup attended was training for the corporate sector. None of it sunk in. Well, that was not entirely true. He could recount the information that he had been taught but, seemingly, could not apply the theory to practical situations. To his parents he was a dullard and would only progress with a great deal of luck. \
When adolescents reach the age of 16 they are introduced to mind-dump technology as a part of the school curriculum. By wearing what appeared to be an ordinary pair of glasses, but connected to the school data-base, a student could instantly transfer vast amounts of information directly into longterm memory. Student progress accelerated exponentially, but it did not make a student any smarter, it just gave them more information for there existing intellect to draw upon and process as best as their minds could. The degree to which the information was retained varied from student to student, but Shakti\'92s brain refused to retain any data dumped into his uniquely tiny mind. He was the only student in who had ever completely rejected mind-dumps. He was promptly shipped off for medical examinations, such was the shock and embarrassment the news caused his mother. \
Testing of his brain function showed the information was dumped his brain in the normal and expected fashion, but immediately began to fade and was gone within minutes. Eloise Shakti purchased the best scientific minds in an effort to cure Shakti of his evolutionary curse, but the consensus unanimously agreed upon was devastating. Shakti was an ordinary boy and his brain physiology was incompatible with digital knowledge acceleration. \'93Monkeys retain information better\'94 was said on more than one occasion.\
Returning to school he soon fell behind while his compatriots progressed in leaps and bounds. He was required by the curriculum to undertake all brain-dump sessions which were as painful as they were embarrassing. He was greeted at every session by the sight of a stuffed monkey strapped into his chair and hooked up to the data base. \'93Monkey Boy\'94 would be whispered and the class would roar with laughter, reaching its peak when he gingerly unhooked the monkey and pointlessly took its place. \
Both parents earnestly apprehended the fear that he would end up in the public service and when he applied for law enforcement training it was embarrassment and shame heaped upon humiliation. When they shared their concern with their Level 5 peers, it was met with incredulous facial expression and cries of disbelief. Short term contracting to the public sector was acceptable, but no one at level 5 or above had actually joined the public service. One could only expect, at best, to gain level 5 status after a lengthy and extremely successful career in public office. Without immersing oneself in corruption, there was no way of accumulating personal wealth in the public sector. Not that anyone was all that concerned by a bit of graft, but corrupt practices were expected to remain where they always have flourished \'96 in the private sector. To the management elite, the petty corruptions of public officials were paltry and laughably amateur compared to their own acts of moral turpitude.\
To the police Shakti was a perfect candidate for employment. Most of the police recruits were level 1 or 2 and occasionally a loser from level 3 might apply, but they had never had an application from a level 5 candidate. The recruiting officers were completely dumbfounded when he transmitted his application. In social terms, he outranked all senior executive and was level pegging with the Commissioner. This pleased them greatly and it was with immense satisfaction and diligent zeal that they fast-tracked the application. \
 \
Most of the recruiting processes were aimed at weeding out scoundrels and rapscallions from level 1 and 2. Expecting a level 5 candidate to suffer the ignominy of the entire recruitment nightmare would be frowned upon and be seen by upper society as an attack on them. 
\f5\i Fancy forcing one of our own to undertake those silly tests.
\f1\i0  Besides, what if he failed? As a moral certainty, the entire recruiting section would be canned and the Police Department would move one step closer to total privatisation. The only ordeal he had to undertake was the medical examination. The last thing that the cops wanted was to employ some diseased weakling, level 5 or not. The Senior Recruiting Officer had made one mistake in his career, allowing a recruit with cancer to commence training and he knew, because of this blunder, he would never, ever, make it to level 4. He was not going to make that mistake again. Shakti\'92s health screen revealed no congenital conditions or acquired diseases and he commenced training within days.\
Progression of this candidate was monitored with interest by the Commissioner\'92s Office and very early in his training it was realised that he did not possess any of the qualities generally attributed to the law enforcement officer. This caused much consternation in the staff rooms as they knew that they could not fail this person, but he was simply the worst candidate they had ever seen. Like a pain-in-the-neck mature age student, his only contribution to theory classes was incessantly asking idiotic questions or stating the painfully obvious. Each and every time he opened his stupid gob, his comments were promptly followed by pained groans from his classmates. There was always one in the class, but no one had expected it to be Shakti. 
\f5\i What did they teach in those expensive private schools? 
\f1\i0 They would all ask themselves. 
\f5\i Whatever it was, he must have been sick that day.
\f1\i0 \
He did not meet the physical demands of the program either. He could not shoot \'96 which was a major achievement in itself. The Particle Pulse Pistols (PPP), now on standard issue, had the new \'93tag and bag\'94 firing system. Not unlike the old laser sights on the obsolete gas compression models, the PPP had a unique targeting system. The sights left a unique electro-magnetic signature on the target which was identical to the signature of the next burst from the barrel. After tagging, the trigger is depressed and the projectile (really a short particle beam burst) always hit the tagged target. You just had to point the pistol in the general direction of the target and squeeze the trigger. It was foolproof, but not Shakti-proof. He was unable to tag his target and, consequently, he was also unable to bag anything either. Weapons instructors spent many, many hours on the firing range with Shakti and following failure upon failure, he was passed. He was passed not only because of political and internal pressures, but also for the sake of their own mental health. Getting him the hell out of the training college and, hopefully, the hell out of their lives, became the only means of preserving their collective sanity.\
The entire class of \'9252 hated him without exception. Initially some latched onto him and made aborted attempts at friendship. They figured that a Level 5 was, most probably, going to be fast tracked to promotion and if they got in on the ground floor, they would be dragged up with him. However, even the slimiest sycophants in the class had their limits. They would not place their future in this imbecile\'92s hands. It seemed he had absolutely no laudable qualities whatsoever. \
It took no time at all for Shakti to acquire many unflattering nicknames. Firstly his class mates dubbed him Scott \'96 \'91Scott-no-brains\'92 or \'91Scott-no-hope\'92. Then they moved to \'91Fuck Wit\'92 and finally just \'91F.W.\'92 Even with this derision directed straight at his face he was under the impression that this was how all level one\'92s and two\'92s interacted. He found it a compliment that the common man would treat him as an equal, one of their own. He was happy to have been able to breach the gulf between the higher community and the common as some sort of social pioneer.\
It was not until the final weeks of training that he finally showed aptitude to any policing duties. Not unlike academy dweebs, members of the Public Affairs Bureau were usually fuck-sticks who could not hold their own on the street or who were too scared to try. They kidded themselves into thinking that they were actually real police officers. To the rest of the rank and file, these people were nothing more than pretenders who liked to tell bullshit war stories at parties in order to jag a fuck. The training to deal with the Public Information Outlets involved many mock media interviews, press releases and \'93talking to camera.\'94 Although he could not think on his feet, if the copy was written for him Shakti excelled in this environment. The news resonated throughout the academy. To the Chancellor, this represented the removal of a great weight from his shoulders. He knew how Damocles had felt. The pressure from above had been almost unbearable. Now the dangling sword was gone and he could send Shakti to the Public Affairs Bureau with the honest recommendation that he was suited to this area. \
Shakti became a household name very quickly. His arrival at Public Affair Bureau coincided with the launch of Police Video Streaming. His face was on every Vid-Screen; Vid-board; poster; milk carton, leaflet and some smart-arse even put his face on toilet paper. Although paper products were prohibitively expensive, they sold like hot-cakes, mostly to police officers as it provided an opportunity to do something they had long wanted to do. There were two channels transmitted to levels 1 to 3 and one other channel for the consumption of Level 4 and above. The programming was almost entirely real life docu-dramas and with his flare for publicity and milking it was worth, his show took off. It was the first time in his life that he had useful and, in some cases, interesting ideas that took reality vid-streams to new lows - exactly what was required. Reality TV shows had long since proven that any idiot can be a star and Shakti, even with his advanced level idiocy, gained a level of acclaim.\
Shakti also had the balls, or the na\'efve stupidity, to suggest that a similar show be conceived for the consumption of the lows. He envisioned corporate fraudsters and rogue traders being brought to book live on his program. He would be an icon to the unwashed masses. The show went ahead, but Shakti was not made aware of some of the minor changes. All of the people featured on show were actors or impostors and no level 5\'92s were harmed during the production of any episode. Fueled by total ignorance, Shakti took to his new second show with a fervent enthusiasm. His genuine belief in the integrity of the program gave the fa\'e7ade a credibility that struck a chord with the discontented lows. At last someone was doing something about the morally bankrupt managers and the corporations they serve. \
In reality, anyone caught on the show was a convicted low already on a death sentence. They knew that the lows had an extraordinary and inexplicable attachment to family members and, like vultures, they pecked and tore at this devotion until leverage was found. To ensure they played along the production company promised social promotion and employment for their families. They were going to die anyway and figured that they may as well do some good for the kids before they got zapped. The final carrot dangled in front of the impostor was the possibility, as slim as it might be, that the audience of the lows might vote to spare him or her. Fat chance, they loved their payback. \
Within a few short years Shakti, in his own right, was extremely wealthy and known throughout the PTD as a fighter for justice - a level 5 with the heart of a level 1. Due to the massive amounts of revenue raised by \'93Candid Criminal\'94 and associated programs, Public Safety Television thrived and became the largest operational command, with no less than 3000 workers \'96 techs, locksmiths, arrest teams and, of course, executioners. Promotion to Lieutenant, with the help of his mother\'92s clout and negotiating skills, was a mere formality for Shakti. Anyone who brought in that much revenue was rewarded, notwithstanding the apparently lifeless organ inside his skull. It was never brought to his attention, nor did Shakti ever notice, that the rank of Lieutenant did not previously exist and stood outside the normal rank structure, somewhere to the side, where no-one could work out where he fit in. His mother thought it to be a suitable metaphor for her idiot son\'92s life.
